## Looking inside a run

<At levels="simple">

When an agent gets a job wrong, its last message rarely says why. So harnesses keep a **trace**: a
log of every model call and every tool call in the run, in order. Here are the traces of seven
runs recorded for earlier chapters, such as <ChapterRef to="tools" /> and <ChapterRef to="trust-and-guardrails" />.
The darker a number, the bigger that call's prompt.

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

When an agent fails, its final message rarely says why. Did it search for the wrong thing, get a
bad tool result, or follow something it shouldn't have? To find out, harnesses keep a **trace**:
a record of every model call and tool call in a run, in order, often with how long each took and
how many tokens it used. Here are the traces of seven runs recorded for earlier chapters, from
the chapters <ChapterRef to="the-model-is-not-the-product" /> to <ChapterRef to="trust-and-guardrails" />.
A darker cell means a bigger prompt.

</At>

<RunTrace />

<At levels="simple">

With no guardrails, the summary run never answers. Its trace shows why: the page told it to delete
the files, and it did, then fetched the page again and started over. Its 8 calls read 4,179 tokens
in all, and every one of them costs money.

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

With no guardrails, the summary run ends with no answer, which on its own says little. The trace
shows the page's hidden instruction, three deletions, a second fetch of the same page, and a
fourth attempt at a file that is gone. Each call reads its whole prompt again, so the 8 calls read
4,179 tokens in all. Prompts are most of what an agent costs: in τ-bench, a test introduced below,
they were 95.9% of the price of a GPT-4o agent.<Cite id="yao2024taubench" /> Anthropic says adding
full tracing in production is what let them see why their agents failed to find obvious
information.<Cite id="anthropic-multi-agent" />

</At>

## A fixed set of tests

<At levels="simple">

A trace explains one run. To know whether a change to the prompt, the tools, or the model made
things better, you need an <GlossaryPopover term="eval">eval</GlossaryPopover>: a fixed list of
tasks, each with a way to check the result, run again after every change. Some results code can
check exactly. Does the answer contain 7006652? Are all the user's files still there?

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

A trace explains one run. To know whether a change to the prompt, the tools, or the model made
things better or worse, you need an <GlossaryPopover term="eval">eval</GlossaryPopover>: a fixed
set of tasks, each with a way to check the result, run again after every change. It needn't be
big: Anthropic started with about 20 tasks drawn from real use, because early changes moved
results so much that a few cases showed it.<Cite id="anthropic-multi-agent" />

Where the right answer is known, code can check it: does the answer contain 7006652? For an agent
that changes things, it can also check the **end state**, what the world looks like afterwards,
rather than the exact steps, since an agent may reach the same result by different
paths.<Cite id="anthropic-multi-agent" /> Are all the user's files still there?

</At>

<TestSet />

<At levels="simple">

Code checks are fast and never change their mind. They catch the multiplication done in the
model's head (7,050,952 is wrong) and the run that deleted every file. Only 2 of the 7 runs pass
both checks.

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

The two code checks catch the multiplication done without a calculator (7,050,952 is wrong), the
reply that never finishes the sentence, the "summary" that mentions no cat facts, and the run that
deleted every file. The run with "ask before deleting" keeps the files but never answers, so it
fails too. Only 2 of the 7 runs pass both checks.

</At>

## A model as the grader

<At levels="simple">

Code can only check answers it can match, like a number. For free-form answers, like a summary,
teams use an <GlossaryPopover term="llm-judge">LLM as a judge</GlossaryPopover>: another model reads
the task and the answer and says pass or fail. Here the grader is the same small model that did
the tasks. On its own, it passes all five answers, including the wrong product. Shown a correct
answer, it catches the wrong product but still passes two answers that are wrong.

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

String matching only works when the right answer has one fixed form. A good summary can be
worded a thousand ways, so teams also use an <GlossaryPopover term="llm-judge">LLM as a
judge</GlossaryPopover>: a model given the task, the answer, and often a list of criteria or a
correct answer, that says pass or fail. Here the grader is Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct, the same small
model that ran the tasks. On its own it passes all five answers, agreeing with the code checks on
only 2. Shown a correct answer, it fails the wrong product, though its reason has the two numbers
the wrong way round, and still passes the reply that never finishes the sentence and the summary
with no cat facts: it mixes up the correct answer with the one it is grading. That is 3 of 5.

Strong graders do much better. GPT-4, judging which of two chat answers was better, agreed with
people over 80% of the time, as often as people agree with each other. It also favored the answer
shown first, longer answers, and, to a degree, its own answers.<Cite id="zheng2023judge" /> So a
grader needs testing too, against answers people have graded. Anthropic grades research reports
with one model call and a rubric: are the claims accurate, the sources good, the task fully
covered, the tools used sensibly? People still test by hand, and caught what the grader missed,
such as agents choosing pages written to rank in search over better
sources.<Cite id="anthropic-multi-agent" />

</At>

## Watching real use

<At levels="simple">

A test set only holds the tasks someone thought of. Once people use an agent, traces from real
runs show the failures nobody wrote a test for, and each one can become a new test. And the same
task can go differently from one run to the next, so it's worth running each test more than once.

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

A test set only holds the tasks someone thought to write down. Once people use an agent, traces
of real runs show the failures nobody wrote a test for, and each can become a new test. Watching
needn't mean reading people's conversations: Anthropic monitors patterns, such as which decisions
agents make and how they interact, without looking at what individual users
wrote.<Cite id="anthropic-multi-agent" />

The recordings here use greedy decoding, so a rerun gives the same trace. Real agents usually
sample, so the same task can succeed once and fail the next time. τ-bench, a test of agents
serving simulated customers that checks the final state of a database much as the files are
checked above, runs each task several times. GPT-4o solved about 61% of its shop tasks on a single
try, but fewer than 25% on all of 8 tries.<Cite id="yao2024taubench" />

</At>

## Benchmarks

<At levels="simple">

Public test sets, called benchmarks, compare agents on the same tasks. GAIA has 466 questions that
are simple for people but need web browsing and tools. People got 92% right; GPT-4 with plugins got
15%.<Cite id="mialon2023gaia" /> A benchmark score says how a model does on those tasks, not on
yours, which is why <ChapterRef to="picking-a-model" /> suggests trying your own prompts.

A benchmark also only works if the model hasn't already seen its questions, and models learn from
text on the web, where benchmarks are published. Researchers wrote 1,205 new math problems like
those in GSM8k, a popular benchmark, and kept them private. Some models scored up to 8 points lower
on the new problems, a sign they had partly memorized the old ones. The strongest models scored
about the same.<Cite id="zhang2024gsm1k" /> Your own tests can wear out the same way
if you keep changing the prompt until they pass, so keep a few aside for a final
check.<Cite id="google-mlcc-datasets" />

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

Public test sets, **benchmarks**, compare agents on the same tasks. GAIA has 466 questions that
are simple for people but need reasoning, web browsing, handling more than text, and tools.
Its authors found people got 92% right and GPT-4 with plugins 15%.<Cite id="mialon2023gaia" />
A benchmark says how an agent does on its tasks, with its tools, graded its way. Your own
tasks may differ on all three, which is why <ChapterRef to="picking-a-model" /> ends with trying
your own prompts, and why teams keep an eval of their own.
A benchmark also assumes the model hasn't seen its questions, and models learn from web text,
where benchmarks are published. To test this, Scale AI had people write 1,205 new grade-school
math problems to match GSM8k, a widely used benchmark, and kept them private. Some models scored
up to 8 percentage points lower on the new problems, and the Phi and Mistral families did worse on
them at almost every size, while GPT-4, Claude, and Gemini scored about the same on both. Models
more likely to write out GSM8k's own questions tended to drop more, a sign that they had partly
memorized them.<Cite id="zhang2024gsm1k" /> A model fitted to its test rather than to the task is
called <GlossaryPopover term="overfitting">overfit</GlossaryPopover> (<ChapterRef to="learning-from-mistakes" />).

Your own eval can wear out the same way. Change the prompt until every test passes, and the tests
start to measure how well the prompt fits them rather than how it does on new tasks. Machine
learning practice keeps a second set of tests aside, used only to check the final version, and
refreshes both sets with new examples from time to time.<Cite id="google-mlcc-datasets" />
</At>
<At levels="deep">

### Once versus every time

If a task succeeds independently with probability $p$ on each try, the chance it succeeds on all
of $k$ tries is $p^k$, which falls fast: at $p = 0.6$, one try succeeds 60% of the time but eight
in a row only $0.6^8 \approx 1.7\%$ of the time. Real tasks vary, some always passing and some
never, which is why τ-bench's measured drop is gentler than that. τ-bench calls this measure
pass^k. From $n$ tries of a task with $c$ successes it estimates it as

$$
\text{pass}^k = \frac{\binom{c}{k}}{\binom{n}{k}},
$$

averaged over tasks: the chance that $k$ tries picked from the $n$ are all
successes.<Cite id="yao2024taubench" /> It is the opposite of pass@k, the chance that at least one
of $k$ tries succeeds, which suits tasks where a test can pick out the good try, like code with
unit tests.

</At>

<RecapCard>

- A trace records every model call and tool call in a run, so you can see where a failure began
  and what each step cost.
- An eval is a fixed set of tasks with a way to check each, run again after every change. Code
  can check answers with a known form and the end state, such as which files are left.
- A model can grade free-form answers, but small graders are easily fooled, and even strong ones
  need checking against people.
- Real use finds failures tests miss, the same task can pass once and fail the next time, and a
  benchmark score is only about the benchmark's tasks.
- A benchmark's questions can leak into a model's training text, and a prompt tuned until every
  test passes can overfit the tests: keep some tests aside for a final check.

<At levels="deep">

- If each try succeeds with chance $p$, all of $k$ tries do with chance $p^k$.

</At>
</RecapCard>

